# ADR-0002: VDI desktop (KasmVNC) as a Coder app through the middle bounce

- **Status:** Proposed — POC validated locally (2026-10-21), not yet on EKS/UBI
- **Date:** 2026-10-22
- **Scope:** Workspace access UX (portal → middle → Coder), workspace image/template design, retirement of ttyd and of subdomain-app/wildcard-DNS requirements for desktop workspaces

## Context

Our access model so far (see `spike-middle/docs/bounce-for-all-coder-apps.md`):

- Users never log into the Coder dashboard. The portal asks the middle for a one-time
  code and form-POSTs it to the bounce (`POST /v1/bounce/start` → `/_portal/bounce`),
  which sets an app-only Coder key in a narrowly scoped cookie.
- Each tool is a separate `coder_app` opened in its own browser tab (VS Code Browser,
  JupyterLab, …).
- The terminal is **ttyd as a path app**, because Coder's built-in web terminal needs
  the user's full key in a `Path=/` cookie (bounce doc §3.7).
- Some local web tools only work as **subdomain apps** (e.g. Airflow running inside
  the workspace, `templates/jupyter-subdomain/main.tf`: `airflow standalone` on
  `127.0.0.1:8080` with `subdomain = true`). Subdomain apps require wildcard DNS,
  a wildcard certificate, load-balancer rules on the wildcard hosts, and a second
  bounce variant running on the app host (bounce doc §3.10, validated locally only).

Two needs are not well served by this model:

1. **GUI / desktop tools** — IntelliJ and other thick clients, multi-window
   workflows, "I just want my desktop" users. Browser apps cannot cover these.
2. **Operational weight of subdomain apps** — wildcard DNS/TLS and the app-host
   bounce exist largely to serve local web UIs (Airflow, Jupyter) that are already
   listening on `localhost` inside the workspace.

A native RDP daemon (xrdp) was considered for (1) but fits poorly: RDP is raw TCP
(so it cannot ride the HTTP bounce; it would need `coder port-forward`), it wants
root/sesman/PAM/systemd, and it imposes a second, Linux-password login.

## Decision

**Offer a full Linux desktop ("VDI") inside the workspace, delivered as an
ordinary Coder path app through the existing bounce. Everything graphical then
lives inside that desktop.**

| Layer | Choice |
| --- | --- |
| Desktop access | **KasmVNC** via Coder's official registry module (`registry.coder.com/coder/kasmvnc/coder`), XFCE desktop, exposed as `coder_app` slug `kasm-vnc`, **`subdomain = false`** (path app) |
| Entry point | Portal button → `openViaBounce({ purpose: "app", workspace_id, slug: "kasm-vnc" })` — identical recipe to every other app; app-only key in cookie; dashboard unreachable |
| Desktop contents (baked into image) | XFCE, Chromium, VS Code GUI, JupyterLab, (IntelliJ where licensed), terminal |
| Local web tools (Airflow, Jupyter, …) | Run on `localhost` in the workspace as today, opened **inside the desktop** via desktop icons — **no `coder_app`, no subdomain, no bounce involvement** |
| Icon launch pattern | XFCE `.desktop` files + wrapper scripts: start the service if needed, then open it in a Chromium app-mode window (`chromium --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --app=http://127.0.0.1:<port>`) so it looks like a native app |
| Terminal | XFCE terminal inside the desktop. **ttyd is retired for desktop workspaces**; Coder's built-in web terminal stays hidden (`web_terminal = false`) |
| Workspace image (target) | UBI 9 + XFCE from EPEL (internal mirror available) + KasmVNC RHEL 9 RPM; apps baked in at build time, never downloaded at workspace start |
| CLI / IDE tokens | Unchanged: scoped tokens (`coder:workspaces.access` + `user:read`, ± `operate`) minted by the middle; no `user_secret:*` unless deliberately granted |

### Access flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant P as Portal
  participant M as Middle
  participant B as Bounce (plane host)
  participant C as Coder
  participant W as Workspace (KasmVNC :6800, XFCE)
  P->>M: POST /v1/bounce/start {purpose: app, slug: kasm-vnc}
  M-->>P: {bounce_url, code}
  P->>B: form-POST code (new tab)
  B-->>C: 302 with app-only cookie
  C->>W: /@user/ws.main/apps/kasm-vnc/
  Note over W: Inside desktop: Terminal, VS Code,<br/>Jupyter icon → 127.0.0.1:8888<br/>Airflow icon → 127.0.0.1:8080
```

## What this retires / makes unnecessary (desktop profile)

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| ttyd as the terminal app | XFCE terminal inside the VDI; ttyd removed (may remain in a future "light" no-desktop profile) |
| `coder_app` with `subdomain = true` for local Airflow/Jupyter | No `coder_app` at all; desktop icon opens `127.0.0.1:<port>` |
| Wildcard DNS + wildcard certificate | Not required |
| App-host bounce variant (bounce doc §3.10) + wildcard ALB/Ingress rules | Not required; bounce exists only on the plane host |
| Port-forward UX for local web UIs | Desktop browser opens `localhost:<port>` directly |

Unchanged: plane-host bounce, middle endpoints, scoped CLI/IDE tokens, keeping
users out of the Coder dashboard. Browser `coder_app`s (path mode) may remain as
lightweight shortcuts alongside the desktop; they are no longer the only door.

## Alternatives considered

| Option | Why not (as the primary path) |
| --- | --- |
| **xrdp + XFCE** (native RDP) | Raw TCP — cannot use the bounce; needs `coder port-forward` first; wants root/systemd/PAM and a second OS-password login; fights OpenShift restricted SCC / arbitrary UID. Remains an optional add-on if a native RDP client is mandated. |
| **Subdomain apps + wildcard bounce** (status quo for Airflow) | Works (validated locally) but buys wildcard DNS/TLS, extra LB rules and a second bounce variant for tools that are already on `localhost`. |
| **Browser apps + ttyd only** (status quo) | No answer for IntelliJ/thick clients or multi-window desktop workflows. |
| **GNOME instead of XFCE** | Only Red Hat–supported desktop, but heavy and assumes systemd/logind/Wayland — impractical in a workspace container. XFCE comes from EPEL (community, not Red Hat supported — accepted, §Risks). |
| **JupyterLab Desktop (Electron) instead of browser-in-desktop** | A second, heavy Jupyter install to maintain; Chromium `--app` mode gives the same "native app" feel with one install. |

## POC evidence (2026-10-21, local)

Local Coder v2.36.5, Docker workspaces
(`spike-middle/templates/kasm-desktop/`, `spike-middle/local-subdomain-lab/kasm-desktop-lab.sh`):

- kasmvnc module v1.3.0 created a healthy `kasm-vnc` path app; XFCE desktop
  opened in the browser through Coder app auth.
- Desktop icons launched the real tools: **VS Code GUI 1.143** and
  **JupyterLab 4.6.4** (Chromium app-mode window), verified by screenshots and
  by the Jupyter HTTP endpoint coming up on icon launch.
- Image: Debian bookworm + XFCE + Chromium + VS Code + JupyterLab = **3.31 GB**
  (Debian stands in for UBI locally; package source is the only intended difference).
- Two real bugs found and fixed, both relevant to the UBI build:
  1. The VS Code `.deb` leaves a Microsoft apt repo (`vscode.sources`) without a
     working key in the image → all later `apt-get update` fail (broke the module
     install). Fix: remove that repo after install; updates come from image rebuilds.
  2. Chromium and VS Code need `--no-sandbox` in containers, or windows die silently.
- Cosmetic leftover: desktop icons render as generic document icons (icon
  theme/trusted-launcher metadata) — launchers work.

## Consequences

**Positive**

- One entry pattern for everything: portal → bounce → app. The desktop is not a
  special case in the middle or the frontend.
- Infrastructure gets *simpler* as the offer gets richer: no wildcard DNS/TLS,
  no app-host bounce, no ttyd, no RDP exposure.
- Thick clients (IntelliJ, DB tools) become possible without Coder dashboard
  access or a second login (KasmVNC rides Coder app auth; runs as the workspace
  user, no systemd/root — OpenShift-friendly).
- Local web tools keep their native shape (`/` on localhost) instead of being
  forced under a path prefix or a subdomain.

**Negative / costs**

- Desktop workspaces are heavy: multi-GB image, real RAM/CPU (IntelliJ-class
  use ⇒ 4 GB+), idle desktops burn cluster resources. Needs resource profiles,
  autostop, and possibly concurrency limits.
- The desktop image becomes a maintained product (UBI 9 + EPEL + apps):
  patching cadence and an owning team are required. XFCE/EPEL is not
  Red Hat supported; UBI must stay on 9 (no XFCE in EPEL for EL 10 today).
- GUI sessions do not survive workspace stop/restart (home volume persists
  files/settings only) — must be communicated to users.
- New policy decisions: KasmVNC clipboard and file transfer in/out, session
  recording expectations, desktop idle timeout.

## Open items / next steps

1. Build the **UBI 9 + EPEL** version of the image; confirm CRB deps resolve
   against the internal mirror.
2. Validate on **EKS behind the real bounce** (ALB or Go bounce microservice),
   including the app-only cookie and "dashboard shows login page" check.
3. Add IntelliJ; measure real memory/CPU; define workspace profiles
   (light vs desktop) and autostop defaults.
4. Frontend: "Desktop" choice from `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/access`
   (`slug: kasm-vnc`); update `frontend-guide-bounce-and-endpoints.md`
   (terminal section: VDI supersedes ttyd for desktop workspaces).
5. Airflow inside the desktop: preconfigure auth for `airflow standalone`
   (its generated admin password is not a UX); decide which local tools get icons.
6. Fix desktop icon artwork (trusted launchers / icon theme).
7. Decide clipboard/file-transfer policy for KasmVNC before any pilot.

